<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/morphoeval-delphi10000-drosophila-deflecta-turing-wavelength-s0-overall> a pk:LLMClaimAssessment ;
    rdfs:label "Drosophila deflecta detail-scale Turing wavelength (2.633 µm, pass 1) — MorphoEval overall 81.6/100" ;
    dct:date "2026-10-27"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:description "Pass 1 implements Schnakenberg kinetics and the Turing wavenumber k_c with the length unit pinned so lambda_c = 2.633e-06 m (Holocene, 0 Ma). The growth GIF is the unforced field (12 frames, 640x480). Computer-vision similarity against the gallery tile is 82.9. The D_u sweep is monotonic (1.84 µm to 4.90 µm) and the equal-diffusion ablation is not Turing (amplitude ratio 0.046). Pearson r, RMSE, and KS of a specimen-conditioned field are not yet attached to the rendered GIF. Predecessor D. cyrtoloma detail wavelength 5.024 µm; successor D. elegans 2.447 µm. Overall 81.6/100 (faithfulness 79.7, reproducibility 86.1); rubric morphoeval 0.1.0; checkpoint commit 1e50c4170c6625b5cc147448d874ba7274b4f92c; environment: . Dimension assessments: Model fidelity to source 86, Morphological realism / specimen match 76, Parameter expressivity 88, Morphometric validation 72, Source and data traceability 88, Code-to-model traceability 84, Reproducibility package 86. Output judged: docs/media/drosophila_deflecta_turing_wavelength_s0_comparison.png: rendered output of models/2026-delphi10000-drosophila-deflecta-turing-wavelength-s0.py at 1e50c4170c6625b5cc147448d874ba7274b4f92c, image/png, 328660 B, 1512×984 px, artifact_sha256 c28d2882afca863a31afba3b746fcf2bed7b5d646faf1ec5a4381a58a19fde91 (sha256 c28d2882afca863a31afba3b746fcf2bed7b5d646faf1ec5a4381a58a19fde91), spec_fingerprint f53640c22dddeac046e5584ccab7413ad1ac27173db0c1834e34d0371d9da099. Dual provenance: spec_fingerprint: f53640c22dddeac046e5584ccab7413ad1ac27173db0c1834e34d0371d9da099; artifact_sha256: c28d2882afca863a31afba3b746fcf2bed7b5d646faf1ec5a4381a58a19fde91; input_dataset_sha256: {datasets/drosophila_deflecta_turing_wavelength_s0/drosophila_deflecta_turing_wavelength_s0_reference.json=1ab0abd4ee1055ae..., datasets/drosophila_deflecta_turing_wavelength_s0/drosophila_deflecta_turing_wavelength_s0_specimen.jpg=d7c1f825d40ea128...}. Claim boundary: reported_in_source: Turing (1952) derived the diffusion-driven instability and the critical wavenumber. Schnakenberg (1979) gave the two-species kinetics. | Werner et al. (2010) showed Wingless at vein intersections paints spots in D. guttifera. Prud'homme et al. (2006) showed the D. elegans apical spot is a yellow cis-regulatory novelty.; reconstructed_in_script: Nondimensional parameters (a, b, D_u, D_v) = (0.132, 0.780, 0.061, 3.050) are chosen for this 2.633 µm slot. They are not measurements from those papers. | Detail-tier wavelengths of D. cyrtoloma and D. elegans are the primordium-tier catalog lengths rescaled by 207.6/108.8 and 101.1/108.8 onto 2.633 µm.; transfer_boundary: Wingless vein spots were shown in D. guttifera, not as a 2.633 µm wavelength in D. deflecta. The chronoseries radiation label is the assigned bucket; D. deflecta is a quinaria-group fly.; does_not_claim: Does not claim a wet-lab measurement of a 2.633 µm pigment wavelength on D. deflecta. | Does not claim the pass-1 unforced GIF is a pixel fit to the gallery spiral.." ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/concept/morphoeval-rubric-0.1.0> ;
    pk:assessedBy <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/model/grok-4.7> ;
    pk:assessmentType "morphoeval/0.1.0/overall" ;
    pk:evidenceAgainst "remediation needed: Attach Pearson r, RMSE, and KS of the specimen-conditioned activator to the comparison figure and require r > 0.98.",
        "remediation needed: Re-render the growth GIF from the forced trajectory so the last frame is the ocellus the forcing tracks, then re-score cv_sim_similarity.",
        "remediation needed: Record the equal-diffusion amplitude ratio and the D_u sweep coefficient of variation beside the non-circular specimen mask metrics in the pass-2 sidecar." ;
    pk:hasScore 8.16e-01 ;
    pk:hasVerdict "faithful" .

